      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of hydrogen and oxygen. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and arguably the most important substance on Earth, essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its complexity:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is unique because of how it behaves physically and chemically:\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it transports vital nutrients and minerals in our bodies and in ecosystems.\n* **Three States of Matter:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states: \n  * **Liquid** (oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; it expands and becomes *less* dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **The Oceans:** About **96.5%** of Earth's water is stored in the oceans as saltwater.\n* **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrological (water) cycle. It evaporates from the surface, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow"
